ABSTRACT
Texas stands at the center of the American AI boom—and at the wall it has built. As of
August 2026, the Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) is fielding approximately 474
GW of large-load interconnection requests, more than five times the grid's all-time record
peak demand of 91,089 MW, with roughly 90% originating from data centers. In response,
Governor Greg Abbott has ordered a full audit of the interconnection queue, paused new
large-load energization, and directed the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality to
stop issuing permits for new data centers until the audit is complete.
This paper argues that the Texas energy wall is not a grid-capacity problem. It is an
architecture problem. The workloads driving the queue are built on stochastic, unbounded
inference—a class of computation that cannot be specified, cannot be certified, and cannot
be bounded in energy or execution time. A deterministic alternative exists: ST-T1024, an
open, Apache-2.0-licensed standard for deterministic, agentic AI inference that runs at 3.7
μJ per query, with Assured Execution Time (AET) ≤ 800 ns, passive cooling, and air-gapped
sovereignty.
The paper proposes a four-path implementation plan for Texas: (1) a regulatory sandbox
pilot for independent silicon validation, (2) certification-first deployment in safety-critical
industries, (3) sovereign edge deployment for state agencies and critical infrastructure, and
(4) workforce development through the existing Texas CHIPS Act infrastructure. The claim is
not that ST-T1024 replaces stochastic AI. The claim is that a large fraction of the inference
workloads currently queued for ERCOT interconnection can be served without a grid
connection at all—bypassing the energy wall rather than climbing it.
1. THE PROBLEM: THE TEXAS ENERGY WALL
1.1 The Queue
In August 2026, Governor Abbott disclosed that ERCOT's large-load interconnection queue
had reached 474 GW of requested capacity—more than five times the grid's record peak of
91,089 MW set on July 22, 2026. Approximately 87–90% of those requests are from data
centers. ERCOT's own May 2026 forecast indicated that peak demand could more than
quadruple by 2032.





